#%PAM-1.0
# Nidara lockscreen PAM service configuration
# Installed to /etc/pam.d/nidara-lock
#
# `auth` verifies the credential; `account` says whether the account may be used
# at all (expired, administratively locked, pam_time windows). BOTH are exercised
# by lib/nidara-auth — pam_authenticate() then pam_acct_mgmt(). Until 2026-08-23
# only the first ran, so this file declared checks that never happened.
#
# No `session` stack: the lockscreen authenticates against a session that is
# already open. It never calls pam_open_session()/pam_close_session(), so a
# session stack here would be config nobody runs.
#
# ⚠️ Every unlock logs this, and it is NOT a failure (#478, measured 2026-09-07):
#
#     pam_unix(nidara-lock:account): setuid failed: Operation not permitted
#
# The lockscreen is an unprivileged gjs process, so pam_unix cannot read
# /etc/shadow itself and shells out to `unix_chkpwd`. Its child tries setuid(0)
# first, and when that fails it only gives up if geteuid() == 0 — i.e. if it
# really was root and something is wrong. For an ordinary caller it logs at
# LOG_DEBUG and proceeds to execve(unix_chkpwd), which is setuid root and does
# the reading. The journal entry's PRIORITY is 7; the branch that gives up logs
# at 3 and is followed by an exit. Priority is how you tell them apart.
#
# So the account checks below are real. `scripts/dev/acct-expiry-probe.sh` is
# the proof and re-runs in a minute: healthy → 0, `chage -d 0` → 12, expired
# account → 13. Do not "fix" the log line by dropping the account stack.

auth        include     system-auth
account     include     system-auth
